{"metadata": {"team_id": "TREMA_UNH", "run_id": "run_1-task-2", "topic_id": "msmarco_v2.1_doc_41_1960853470", "type": "automatic", "use_starter_kit": 1}, "responses": [{"text": "According to the 2017 report by the National Academies of Sciences, Engineering and Medicine, their findings on the health effects of cannabis and cannabinoids in relation to cancer are currently limited but promising. The report acknowledges that while there is some evidence supporting the potential anti-cancer properties of these substances, more research is needed to fully understand their mechanisms and efficacy.", "citations": ["msmarco_v2.1_doc_07_1295425681#23_2363755529"]}, {"text": "In a 2015 study published on Medical News Today, researchers identified that THC's anti-tumor properties are linked to the activation of CB1 and CB2 receptors in cells. This mechanism is believed to contribute to the reduction of tumor growth.", "citations": ["msmarco_v2.1_doc_34_224416376#19_479384525"]}, {"text": "The 2018 report by the National Academies of Sciences, Engineering and Medicine found that while there is some evidence suggesting cannabis can induce apoptosis in certain types of cancer cells, more research is required to confirm these findings. The report emphasizes the need for further studies to explore the potential therapeutic benefits of cannabinoids.", "citations": ["msmarco_v2.1_doc_07_1375200787#0_2525847830"]}, {"text": "A study published in Phytomedicine found that a high-CBD cannabis extract inhibited cell proliferation of colorectal cancer and weakened colon carcinogenesis through the activation of CB1 and CB2 receptors. This suggests potential clinical relevance for using cannabis to treat colorectal cancer.", "citations": ["msmarco_v2.1_doc_46_250609978#4_607983812"]}, {"text": "WebMD reports that several studies on animal and human cells, as well as a small study involving 9 people, suggested THC and other cannabinoids might slow the growth of brain cancer. However, no studies in people have taken place to confirm these findings.", "citations": ["msmarco_v2.1_doc_10_670137725#12_1254518322"]}, {"text": "The 2018 report by the National Academies of Sciences, Engineering and Medicine also found that while there is some evidence supporting the use of cannabis for chronic pain related to conditions such as cancer and fibromyalgia, more research is needed to establish its efficacy compared to traditional pain medications.", "citations": ["msmarco_v2.1_doc_22_1426810754#11_3092894408"]}, {"text": "According to the studies mentioned by WebMD, marijuana has shown improvement in pain relief for chronic diseases such as cancer and fibromyalgia when compared to a placebo or other traditional pain medications. However, about a quarter of the studies showed no significant improvement.", "citations": ["msmarco_v2.1_doc_10_670137725#12_1254518322"]}, {"text": "The 2018 report by the National Academies of Sciences, Engineering and Medicine also found that while there is some evidence suggesting cannabis can be used to treat other types of cancers such as breast cancer and leukemia, more research is needed to confirm these findings.", "citations": ["msmarco_v2.1_doc_07_1375200787#0_2525847830"]}]}
